        "BHI street preachers are often extraordinarily well-versed in Scripture — or at least in their selected proof texts. A common tactic is 'Scripture bombing': rapidly citing 10, 15, or 20 verses in succession, each intended to support a BHI doctrine, without pausing for context or response. The psychological effect is overwhelming: the listener feels outmatched and assumes that the sheer volume of verses proves the point.\n\nBut quantity of citations does not equal quality of interpretation. A single verse properly exegeted defeats twenty verses ripped from context. Your tactical response is simple: slow the conversation down. Pick ONE verse from the barrage and say, 'Let's look at that one carefully. Can we read the full chapter together?'\n\nSecond Peter 3:16 warns about those who 'wrest' (twist) Scriptures 'unto their own destruction.' Context is the antidote to proof-texting. When you insist on reading a verse in its full chapter context, two things happen: (1) the verse often says something different from what was claimed, and (2) the tactic of rapid-fire citation is neutralised because you have shifted from quantity to quality.\n\nRemember Proverbs 18:13: 'He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him.' Do not feel pressured to respond to every verse in the barrage. Focus. Depth defeats breadth in biblical interpretation.",
