        "BHI 'camps' function as tight-knit communities with strong social dynamics. When someone begins studying with a BHI group, they experience: (1) Immediate acceptance — 'Welcome home, brother. You've found your family.' (2) Social reinforcement — the group affirms every step toward BHI doctrine. (3) Social punishment — questioning the teaching is met with correction, disappointment, or exclusion. (4) Us-versus-them framing — the outside world (including former churches) is portrayed as Babylon, and leaving the camp is portrayed as spiritual betrayal.\n\nThese are classic high-control group dynamics, and they are not unique to BHI — they appear in many religious and ideological movements. The key indicator is that loyalty to the group becomes a proxy for loyalty to God. When 'leaving the camp' equals 'leaving God,' the group has replaced God as the ultimate authority.\n\nJeremiah 17:5 warns: 'Cursed be the man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm, and whose heart departeth from the LORD.' And Jesus Himself said in Matthew 23:8-10: 'But be not ye called Rabbi: for one is your Master, even Christ; and all ye are brethren... Neither be ye called masters: for one is your Master, even Christ.' No human leader or group should occupy the place of Christ.\n\nAs SDAs, we must also examine ourselves here — any church can develop unhealthy group dynamics. The standard is always Scripture, never social conformity.",
