      instructorVoice:
        "Welcome to Fallacy Week. For the next seven days, you will learn to identify specific logical fallacies that appear repeatedly in BHI argumentation. Recognising fallacies in real time is a crucial combat skill — it allows you to name the error precisely, which is far more effective than simply saying 'that's wrong.'\n\nWe begin with the genetic fallacy: judging the truth or falsity of a claim based on its origin rather than its evidence. In BHI contexts, this takes several forms: 'Christianity is false because Europeans spread it.' 'The KJV is corrupt because King James was white.' 'The Trinity is pagan because Greeks developed the philosophical vocabulary to describe it.' In each case, the origin or source of the claim is attacked, while the actual evidence for or against the claim is ignored.\n\nJohn 7:24 commands: 'Judge not according to the appearance, but judge righteous judgment.' Truth is truth regardless of who teaches it. A mathematical theorem proved by a German, a Chinese, or a Nigerian mathematician is equally valid — because truth is not determined by the identity of the messenger.\n\nThe genetic fallacy is especially potent in BHI rhetoric because it taps into real historical grievances. If Europeans did misuse Christianity (and they did), it FEELS right to reject everything associated with them. But feelings are not logical arguments. The misuse of truth does not invalidate truth itself. This distinction is the antidote to the genetic fallacy.",
