      instructorVoice:
        "The false cause fallacy (cum hoc ergo propter hoc — 'with this, therefore because of this') occurs when someone assumes that because two things correlate, one must have caused the other. In BHI theology, this manifests as: 'Deuteronomy 28 describes suffering X. Group Y experiences suffering X. Therefore, Group Y must be the Israelites to whom Deuteronomy 28 was addressed.'\n\nThe logical structure is: (1) The Bible predicts suffering S for people P. (2) Group G experiences suffering S. (3) Therefore, Group G must be people P. But this conclusion does not follow, because many groups throughout history have experienced similar sufferings. Slavery, dispersion, cultural erasure, and poverty are tragically common in human history — they are not unique to any one people.\n\nConsider: the Romani people have been enslaved, dispersed, persecuted, and subjected to genocide. The Armenian people experienced genocide and diaspora. The Irish experienced famine, forced emigration, and cultural suppression. Could each of these groups equally claim to be the 'true Israelites' based on Deuteronomy 28? The answer exposes the fallacy.\n\nFirst Corinthians 2:13 instructs us to compare 'spiritual things with spiritual' — that is, to interpret Scripture with Scripture, not to impose external narratives onto the text. The Bible interprets its own prophecies through historical fulfillment, not through modern racial mapping.",
