      instructorVoice:
        "Equivocation is the fallacy of using a word with multiple meanings as though it means the same thing throughout an argument. In BHI theology, the word 'Israel' is the prime candidate. The Bible uses 'Israel' in at least four different senses: (1) Jacob the individual patriarch (Genesis 32:28). (2) The twelve tribes as a nation (Exodus 19:3). (3) The northern kingdom after the split (1 Kings 12:19). (4) The spiritual people of God defined by faith (Galatians 6:16, Romans 9:6).\n\nBHI argumentation frequently equivocates between these senses. When citing Old Testament promises to 'Israel' (the nation), they apply them to a specific modern racial group. When citing New Testament references to 'Israel' (the spiritual people), they insist the term still means a racial group. This equivocation allows them to claim all biblical promises for their group while rejecting the New Testament redefinition of the term.\n\nGalatians 6:16 is crucial: 'And as many as walk according to this rule, peace be on them, and mercy, and upon the Israel of God.' Paul uses 'Israel of God' to describe the community of faith — a usage that transcends ethnic boundaries. Romans 9:6 is equally clear: 'For they are not all Israel, which are of Israel.' Paul explicitly says that biological descent from Israel does not make someone truly 'Israel' in God's sight.\n\nTo detect equivocation, always ask: 'Which sense of Israel are you using in this verse? And is that the same sense the author intended?'",
